WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.250
- [Narrator] During the
boom of climate investing,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.230
there's a surprising
industry that's expected

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.910
to rake in the green.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:06.783
It's garbage.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.110
North America's biggest landfill operator,

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.420
which manages your waste, is known as...

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:13.350
- WM.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.180
- [Narrator] It's best
known for collecting trash

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.710
and putting it in a landfill.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.210
- If you've lived in America

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.410
and you've thrown something away,

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.810
chances are they have handled your trash.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.580
- [Narrator] But beginning in 2026,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.070
the company says
sustainability focused ventures

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.290
will reach 15% of its total profit

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.050
or about $740 million a year.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.940
Here's how the company plans to profit

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.040
from government tax credits and a market

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.073
that's hungry for renewable
energy and recycled materials.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.450
This is the economics of WM.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:46.800
Over the next four years, WM
plans to invest $1.2 billion

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.230
into making fuel from rotting garbage.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.870
When the trash that ends up
in a landfill decomposes,

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.240
it releases a slew of gases.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.960
One of them is methane, known
commercially as natural gas,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.580
which is more environmentally harmful

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.890
to the atmosphere than carbon dioxide.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.290
Landfill owners would
previously capture methane

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.610
and burn it off to prevent it

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.260
from seeping into the atmosphere.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.080
Now, they collect the gases

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.970
and nearby plants separate the methane,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.920
which is piped into power
plants for electricity

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.050
or into homes for heating and cooking.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.960
WM already operates 66 landfill gas plants

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.280
all over the country.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.110
Landfill gas is much more expensive

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.810
to produce than gas drilled
by energy companies.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.250
But thanks to federal tax breaks

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.290
and other incentives to reduce pollution,

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.660
landfill gas has become
economically viable

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.370
for producers and buyers.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.820
WM expects to receive $250
million in tax credits

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.370
installing gasworks at
18 of its landfills.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.890
Thanks to last year's climate,
tax, and healthcare bill.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.110
- The climate bill really was a bit

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.120
more of a catalyst to do it faster.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.160
- Waste Management is
pursuing these projects,

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.770
because they're immensely
profitable right now,

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.290
and it's made projects
that's smaller landfills,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.660
which will be less productive
than large landfills.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.210
It's brought them into the
money, they're now economical.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.710
- [Narrator] WM says this expansion

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.080
will allow it to produce
eight times more gas

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.020
than it does now, and
will generate $500 million

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.360
in additional profit through 2026,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.130
not counting the tax breaks.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.740
Currently, natural gas from
landfills only accounts

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.770
for less than 1% of the
US natural gas supply.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.300
But because of the growing demand,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.640
the number of renewable
natural gas projects,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:18.780
including landfills,

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.970
has nearly doubled in the past five years,

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.520
and production has
significantly increased.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.680
WM's competitors are also cashing in

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.960
with their own landfill gas plants.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.340
Republic Services is planning

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.370
to open a total of 77
renewable energy projects,

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.800
and reported that about
88% of its landfills

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.650
are covered in biogas collection systems.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.560
But WM is not just investing in landfills.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.140
The Waste Management company
is making large investments

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:44.970
in upgrading its recycling plants,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.980
like this one outside of Cleveland.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.720
- Our 100,000 square foot
facility is built to manage

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.120
12,000 tons a month of recycling.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.740
- [Narrator] These
investments are helping plants

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.840
to increase recycling recovery rates,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.170
and the company expects the
new tech to add $240 million

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.480
to its bottom line over
the next four years.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.460
- The WM Cleveland facility prior

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.170
was a commercial recycling plant,

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.620
so we could not offer single
stream commercial recycling.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.040
This investment now has
opened up our capacity

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.590
to both residential and
commercial recycling.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.470
We're able to provide single
stream recycling for them.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:20.790
- [Narrator] One of WM's upgrades

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:22.680
involves automating its recycling plants

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.110
with optical sorters powered by AI.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.170
- So behind me is one
of 12 optical scanners

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.790
within our process system.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.980
The optical scanners are made to pick

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.350
900 to 1,000 picks per minute.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.870
Through programming and imaging,

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.640
it identifies a specific product

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.130
and there's an air jet that
then identifies that product

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.590
onto a conveyor over to
the baler to be bailed

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.550
for the end user.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.710
- It makes the sorting go a lot faster.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:47.880
It removes human air,

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.160
and what they found is
that it really boosts

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.420
not only the quantity

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.790
of the what they call recovered materials,

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.170
but they're getting a better quality.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.810
It's cleaner, it's not a
jumble of other stuff in there.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.090
So they're spending a
lot of money to automate

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.420
these recycling facilities
in order to boost efficiency.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.520
- Where we have made these investments

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.580
we're lowering our
operating costs by reducing

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.010
some of the labor
intensity of the business.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.720
- All that stuff goes to these facilities

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.760
where it's sorted into
different materials,

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.390
you know, mixed paper, corrugated
cardboard, clear bottles,

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:19.830
that all gets separated.

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:23.070
That's a very intense labor,
not a very pleasant job.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.480
These companies have a really hard time

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.460
keeping people in those jobs.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.500
- [Narrator] And these optical
sorters have already begun

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.820
to increase revenue.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.860
Recyclables have become an increasingly

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.390
valuable commodity themselves

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.450
as more companies promise
to use recycled packaging.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.250
- There's a big shortage of the materials

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.350
of recycled material that companies

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.930
like Coca-Cola, or Amazon,

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.660
or whoever uses a lot of plastic and paper

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.400
can reuse for new packaging.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.680
So the race is on to really get that stuff

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.710
out of the waste stream, and
companies are willing to pay up

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.200
for those materials even
though they're more expensive,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.050
because they've told their
investors and their customers

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:59.430
they're gonna clean up their act.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.980
- [Narrator] Plus, more
cleanly sorted recyclables

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.330
fetch a higher price.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.300
WM says the optical sorters
are able to increase

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.540
blended commodity value
by about 15% per ton,

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.220
and the company expects that it'll be able

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.600
to recover 25% more
reusable materials by 2025.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.660
- We're continuously
looking at that algorithm

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.630
to make sure that we capture
more material long term.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.250
- [Narrator] And
sustainability investments

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.130
are even going back to one
of WM's core businesses.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:27.123
Collections.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:34.230
- People need to do the
job right at the start,

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:37.380
and we have a technology that we use

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.380
called smart truck that
helps us with that.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.600
- [Narrator] Smart trucks
come equipped with cameras.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.030
When the truck tips
recyclables into its hopper,

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.190
it's tech captures
footage of the materials

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:48.990
for a team of technicians to review.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.210
These technicians scan for any objects

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.440
that can't be recycled

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.110
and monitor the amount of recyclables.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.860
If WM detects misplaced
recyclables or overflowing bins,

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.990
it sends a notification to its commercial

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.840
and residential customers
informing them of the issue.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.770
- In our regions where we have
that smart truck technology,

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:12.770
we've seen residue reduce
roughly 20% in those markets,

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:13.883
so that's key.

00:06:13.883 --> 00:06:18.690
Let's just say a really dirty
pizza box mixed in with paper.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.390
You know, that good
paper or good cardboard

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:24.900
gets contaminated and
then it can't be recycled.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.070
- [Narrator] Much of this technology

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.690
is already in place today.

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:30.810
- We definitely think about the materials

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.420
that are in our landfills as just that,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.610
materials, they're a resource.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:36.990
- What we think of as trash,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.480
a lot of it is really some
of the hottest commodities

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.877
of the green economy.

00:06:40.877 --> 00:06:43.460
(gentle music)

